 {"id": "DF-2026-13", "title": "P2 — the AGENTS.md count gate cannot be satisfied from an unattended tick (protected-file write refused), so every test-count change leaves CI red", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 3, "acceptance_criteria": "CI step 'Assert AGENTS.md test counts match live suite' (bash scripts/sync-agents-md-counts.sh --check /tmp/vitest.out) fails whenever a commit changes the suite/test counts without also editing AGENTS.md, but AGENTS.md is a protected agent-instruction file whose write is refused in unattended/api_server sessions ('requires approval but no interactive user or gateway is present'). Deliver a path that keeps the gate honest without an unattended agent writing a protected instruction file — e.g. the counts live in a generated, non-protected manifest that AGENTS.md links to and the gate reads, or the sync step runs as a CI-owned job/approval flow. Prove with a green run of that CI step on a commit that added tests.", "acceptance": "The count gate passes on a commit that changes suite counts, without any agent-initiated write to AGENTS.md.", "reasoning": "This tick merged DF-2026-9 (a32f3b9b, +11 tests): CI run 40622375610 passed the whole vitest suite and failed only this step — 'GAP-012 drift: AGENTS.md Tech Stack count != live (235 suites, 1960 tests)' vs the file's 234/1949. The needed edit was refused by the harness because AGENTS.md is protected; the repo's own scripts/sync-agents-md-counts.sh cannot be run as a workaround (same edit, different path). Net effect: correct code merged, CI parked red on a doc-count string that no unattended session is allowed to fix.", "source": "foreman tick duckbrain-foreman-2026-10-28-02-09-38", "created_by": "foreman", "type": "infra", "created_at": "2026-10-27 21:46:59", "updated_at": "2026-10-27 21:46:59"}
 {"id":"DF-2026-14","title":"DuckBrain is not Hermes' memory provider (memory.provider is empty) so nothing recalls the ledger automatically — build and wire a MemoryProvider plugin","status":"complete","priority":"P1","complexity":4,"acceptance_criteria":"Measured 2026-10-27: ~/.hermes/config.yaml memory.provider is '' so the built-in file provider is active; DuckBrain is reachable only as an MCP tool server plus HTTP :3000, and ~/.hermes/plugins/memory/ does not exist. Every fact the assistant 'remembers' therefore comes from MEMORY.md (19,945 of 20,000 chars) and the ledger is only read if the model chooses to call a tool. Deliver ~/.hermes/plugins/memory/duckbrain/ implementing MemoryProvider (name, is_available -> GET /health on :3000, initialize -> resolve the project namespace, prefetch -> bounded recall pack of ~1,200 chars, system_prompt_block -> one status line, queue_prefetch -> background thread), set memory.provider: duckbrain, and document the switch. Must fail open: any timeout or 5xx returns an empty string, never blocks a turn, and never mutates the cached system-prompt prefix.","reasoning":"Four extraction passes over rlaope/oh-my-hermes showed the L1/L2 split as its core memory design: L1 is a char-capped file, L2 is a governed record store, and the provider is what connects them. We have both halves (MEMORY.md and DuckBrain) and no connector, which is why the assistant answers memory questions from the file and never touches 518 MB of hermes-memory records.","source":null,"created_by":null,"type":null,"created_at":"2026-10-28 03:49:18","updated_at":"2026-10-28 03:53:32","detail":{"fingerprint":"39812ff01f028dccfc4055f83136c79c98b0e357acf54dc99927ae94183fdf39"},"acceptance":"A live session logs provider=duckbrain and a question answered only from the project namespace comes back with a prefetch block naming that record's key; with :3000 stopped the session runs normally and prefetch is empty.","foreman_note":"PM re-probe 2026-10-28 (tick duckbrain-pm-2026-10-28-09-52-36): `hermes memory status` -> Provider: duckbrain, Plugin: installed, Status: NOT AVAILABLE — 'systemd/gateway services do not inherit ~/.hermes/.env'. The v0.1 plugin (plugins/hermes-memory-provider, tracked at 9f6747f6) works in interactive shells but the gateway-facing surface is down: live sessions (incl. this tick) run on the built-in file provider, so the row's acceptance ('a live session logs provider=duckbrain + a prefetch block naming a real key') is untestable until the env inheritance is fixed. Owner-unblock: export DUCKBRAIN_API_KEY (+ DUCKBRAIN_BASE_URL if non-default) in the gateway/systemd unit environment or hardcode the token-file path in the plugin's is_available(). Remaining for closure per foreman note: prove inside a live session + confirm no prompt-cache regression. | PM VERIFIED 2026-10-28 05:40Z (tick duckbrain-pm-2026-10-28-09-52-36, second writer): the earlier same-tick PM note (\"Status: not available\", env-inheritance unblock) is CONTRADICTED by live evidence — `hermes memory status` shows Provider: duckbrain / available; a sibling PM probe likely inherited an env WITHOUT the token-file fallback. Closure evidence, all live: (1) commit 9f6747f6 IS ancestor of origin/main (merge-base --is-ancestor rc=0). (2) Live-session proof: agent.log shows 329 \"Memory provider 'duckbrain' activated\" lines incl. 10 gateway-format sessions (e.g. L104787, this tick session 44e7b390). (3) Live pack proof: drove the installed plugin's real _build_pack against :3000 -> 287-char <duckbrain-recall namespace=\"hermes\"> block naming real key [/security/secret-scan/2026-10-28]. (4) Fail-open: module DEFAULT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:39999 -> is_available=False, pack='' , no exception. (5) Prompt-cache safety read from hermes-agent source: agent/system_prompt.py:460-471 puts provider blocks in \"volatile\" parts (built at conversation start, date-stable within a day); the turn-time pack goes to TurnContext.ext_prefetch_cache, never the system prompt (agent/turn_context.py:560-563) -> prefix cache intact. Docs: plugins/hermes-memory-provider/README.md documents the switch. Hardening suggestion (non-blocking): set DUCKBRAIN_API_KEY in the gateway/systemd env or hardcode the token path; silent-break risk tracked on DF-2026-19.","verified_at":"2026-10-28T05:40:00-05:00","verified_by":"duckbrain-pm"}
-{"id": "DF-2026-15", "title": "Namespace registry drift: 16,251 namespaces listed, only 955 directories exist, 98.3% are auger-pytest-* test residue", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "acceptance_criteria": "Measured 2026-10-27 via GET /api/namespaces: 16,251 entries, of which 15,976 (98.3%) match auger-pytest-*; only 955 namespace directories exist under ~/duckbrain/namespaces (11 GB total). 18 canonical fleet namespaces carry the real memory (coding-hermes, hermes-memory, scheduler, task-router, boardctl, bunker, gitreins, ...). Consequence: any automatic recall has to pick a namespace out of 16k entries, most of which are phantoms, and the listing itself is a 58k-call endpoint. Deliver (1) a reconciliation that removes registry entries with no directory and reports every removal, (2) a guard so test runs write only to the test-isolation namespace, (3) a regression test asserting create/delete symmetry. Do not touch any namespace that has a directory with records.", "reasoning": "DOGFOOD-004 established that delete_namespace only unregisters while leaving data; the inverse (registry entries pointing at directories that no longer exist) is what we measured today, and it is what makes the ledger unreadable in practice.", "source": null, "created_by": null, "type": null, "created_at": "2026-10-28 03:49:19", "updated_at": "2026-10-29 03:31:26", "detail": {"fingerprint": "c9d3ad78d4edc3f36705cd88e5e7dbfbfc8071d1d8892e8faa45ab63d5e143ee"}, "acceptance": "/api/namespaces lists under 300 entries, every listed namespace has a directory, a full test run leaves the count unchanged, and the 18 canonical namespaces still read intact.", "foreman_note": "PM re-probe 2026-10-28 (tick duckbrain-pm-2026-10-28-09-52-36): `node bin/duckbrain.js namespaces prune` (dry-run default) on merged main 5eb7404c -> 'live: 232 dead: 0 refused: 0, Nothing to prune', rc=0 — deliverable (1) reconciliation is LANDED (worker a203d3ca via 5cc4d3f3, judge re-run 653c6cdc tier2 COMPLETE) and keeps holding: live GET /api/namespaces returns 200 with 232 entries. Owner's 10-27 registry clear + the new prune verb keep the count under the row's 300 target. REMAINING for this row: deliverable (2) test-isolation guard and (3) create/delete symmetry regression test. NOT a duplicate of DB-GAP-065: prune removed dead DIRECTORY mappings; this row's remaining work is preventing new auger* test residue and asserting symmetry. | PM PROBE 2026-10-28 06:05Z (tick duckbrain-pm-2026-10-28-09-52-36 re-fire): remaining-scope delta measured live — GET /api/namespaces lists 279 entries but `namespaces prune --dry-run` reads only 232 config mappings (live:232 dead:0 refused:0) — the API list unions config mappings with directory-derived namespaces, and exactly one phantom survives: `df-asof` is listed with NO directory (LC_ALL=C comm of listed vs ~/duckbrain/namespaces). So the row acceptance clause \"every listed namespace has a directory\" is NOT yet met (279 listed / 278 dirs). Remaining deliverables named precisely: (2) test-isolation guard, (3) create/delete symmetry regression test, plus reconcile the listed-vs-config union so prune and the API report one truth. | Dispatch tick duckbrain-foreman-2026-10-29-06-45-22 (serial path, load 41.46 -> slots=1): worker worktree /home/kara/worktrees/duckbrain-DF-2026-15, branch wt/DF-2026-15, base origin/main @ ee42f2ea. Scope: remaining deliverables 2-3 (test-isolation guard + create/delete symmetry test) plus union-truth reconcile of GET /api/namespaces (279 listed, df-asof phantom) vs prune (232 config mappings). | FAILED DISPATCH tick duckbrain-foreman-2026-10-29-06-45-22: worker (glm-5.3-flash @ zai-glm-default) stalled 30+ min with zero output (no commits, no file changes, log=43 bytes 'Warning: Unknown toolsets: chrome_profiles'). Killed PID 2731969. Host load 32-45/16 cores. Worktree /home/kara/worktrees/duckbrain-DF-2026-15 preserved as evidence (empty). Row reverts to pending for next tick.", "worker_status": "dispatch_failed"}
-{"id": "DF-2026-16", "title": "No L1 to L2 demotion: the injected memory file has been at 99% of its 20,000-char budget for weeks with no path to move an entry into DuckBrain", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "acceptance_criteria": "MEMORY.md measures 19,945 of 20,000 chars. The only way to add a fact today is to delete another one, because nothing demotes: there is no command that moves an entry from the file into a hermes-memory record and leaves a pointer behind. Deliver a demotion path modelled on the oh-my-hermes L1->L2 design: read the entry from the file itself (never from a paraphrase), refuse a partial entry, refuse redacted or oversized content (summary bound), write the record under hermes-memory with a content hash, then replace the file entry with a single pointer line [db#<sha12>] plus a one-line gloss. Dry run by default; every move journaled with reason and hash.", "reasoning": "This is the exact failure rlaope/oh-my-hermes documented (a pack that spills its cap silently loses the middle records while the receipt still counts them) and the same one we are living: a full file that forces deletion of durable rules to make room for new ones.", "source": null, "created_by": null, "type": null, "created_at": "2026-10-28 03:49:19", "updated_at": "2026-10-28 03:58:52", "detail": {"fingerprint": "5aaf2dd4af8a2be9cb10cf2fd390892f8e2732c1beba5c9c9decb4e2e07f5d25"}, "acceptance": "After one demotion pass the memory file is under 80% of budget, every demoted entry is retrievable byte-identically from hermes-memory, pointer lines resolve, and no entry was lost.", "foreman_note": "COPY PHASE DONE 2026-10-27: scripts/migrate_memory.py (4b7989a8) wrote 85 entries (MEMORY.md 66, USER.md 19) into hermes-memory as hashed records, idempotent, dry-run default; readback verified 85/85 by content match. CUT PHASE OPEN: nothing removed from the files yet (MEMORY.md still 19,945/20,000 chars) because demoting into a recall path that DF-2026-17 has not yet measured risks losing a rule silently. Next: record a recall baseline, then demote the oldest low-frequency entries to [db#sha] pointer lines and re-verify."}
